a government white paper on social care  was released this week promising to  spend more than five billion pounds over  three years boris johnson's failed to  end the often crippling builds faced by  many in old age announcing a cap on care  costs for adults in england from october  2023  so what could this mean for the social  care sector  the chief executive of the four seasons  healthcare group jeremy richardson  is with us good morning mr richardson um  how worried is the care sector about uh  the facing a winter crisis with omicron  and everything else  morning trevor i think the secretary is  really worried at the moment not  necessarily because of the threat faced  by omicron but because of the bigger  challenges the wider issues that we've  got which are long term and not just in  the moment  so you think that there could well be a  winter crisis um  as bad as it was last winter  well look i don't think the sector is  going to collapse tomorrow but it's  gradually falling over and the problem  is that incrementally you're seeing a  decline in the quality of services so so  if we look at the social care reform  that's just been announced  you know i applaud the intent that the  government has undertaken because they  are the first government in many decades  to try to tackle the problem  the issue is that the execution is  really disappointing somebody last week  described it as an underpowered saloon  car when what's required is a formula  one racing car and i think that's  probably quite apt  i want to come to the social care  reforms in a second but uh before we do  that can i just ask you about the impact  of the government's mandate uh that all  staff should be jabbed i know that you  and  some of your colleagues were concerned  that this would have an impact on staff  numbers what do you know so far  well look i've been really clear and  i've said i didn't think that mandating  the vaccine was the right thing to do  and i still think that that's the case  and unfortunately that ship has now  sailed um  we have lost about 200 staff members so  that's 200 really hard working members  of team who are providing care to our  residents on the day today  if i may ask  200 people we think have left the  business so 49 people left on the 11th  of november so those were the people  that we had to dismiss but then you've  got the title of what number what's what  is your workforce so we have 12 and a  half thousand team members 49 left on  the final day but we estimate that 200  left in the run-up so in total we've  lost about 1.6 of our workforce and what  you've got to bear in mind is that at  the time that we had to get rid of those  people we already had 95 of our team  fully vaccinated double jabbed providing  protection to themselves and also to the  residents so i think in a situation like  that what you have to do is weigh up  relative risk and the greater risk to my  residents today is that i don't have  enough team members to look after them  and provide really good quality care  than the risk presented by transmission  of the virus itself  so let's talk about the social care  reform to see whether  what the government's brought forward  might help  to  deal with that risk from what you said  earlier on uh you don't think much much  of it uh it clearly didn't meet your  expectations  well no i mean as i say i applaud the  intent so so the prime minister has  stood up and he's told the public that  the social care issues are going to be  solved and if you're a member of the  public then you rightly believe that the  problem has been sold because that's  what the prime minister has told you  that the problem is that all that's been  announced is a short-term funding plan  for the nhs so 36 billion pounds is  going to be raised over the next three  years  31.6 billion of that is going into the  nhs so only 5.4 billion is going into  social care that's 1.8 billion a year  and it's widely accepted by all experts  that the sector needs between seven to  ten billion a year just to stand still  so unfortunately if you're going to  announce that you're fixing social care  don't just fund the nhs there isn't  enough funding going into the sector  that's one of the problems  and the second issue is that it's not  tackling today's issues the money that  we are going to receive in the bulk is  coming in three years time and that's  assuming that the nhs choose to hand it  back over at that stage  uh if i may i when i spoke to mr sunak  some weeks ago about this his argument  was that you couldn't you couldn't spend  that money tomorrow and what you needed  to do was to build it up so that in  three years time you're getting the the  bulk of it and that over time that that  number would build up um  did you accept that argument  no of course i don't i mean that's a  really convenient thing for him to say  because that it allows him to justify  not putting the funding into social care  today that it really needs  so so no i absolutely disagree with that  the fundamental issue here is that the  true cost of care so what it costs to  look after people safely and to provide  them with a really good quality of life  is not provided by the local authorities  so if additional funding came into the  sector now what you would see is an  increase in the average weekly fee  provided by local authorities and i  absolutely could spend that money  because the first thing i do is i'd  start to pay the team who are working so  hard in our homes what they're really  worth at the moment is very difficult  for us to do that because our revenues  are capped by local authorities which in  turn are capped by the lack of funding  provided by central government  i mean all round it sounds like uh you  are really rather despairing  both of the current situation  and also the impact of the government's  uh  plans as published in in their white  paper  well look i'm not despairing because we  are very fortunate at both as a company  and as a country that we have so many  phenomenally hard-working people in  social care i'm despairing beca but then  i am despairing in the context of the  finances because i don't think we've  solved the issues  and we will simply continue to see a  deterioration and gradual erosion if  we're not careful of the services that  we're able to deliver to people and i  think we have to ask ourselves as a  country you know what is it that's  important to us do we want to give our  elderly people dignity and quality of  life in their old age or do we want to  simply ignore them and i think the risk  is that unless we put proper funding  into the sector unless we really grapple  with the issue then we're just going to  see a slow deterioration of the quality  of service we provide and i'm more i  don't think that's morally right i think  we we owe it to our elderly people to  provide the best quality of care that we  possibly can  thank you very much indeed for your time  this morning uh mr richardson  thank you very much
 

